    most, so it's a first-class control rather than a checkbox.
  - [1mFiltering[0m: status filter and a "due soon" view. No search in v1.
  - [1mOverdue[0m tasks are visually distinct. This is the one place the app hints at its future: "due date passed" is a
    change that happens with no user action, which is precisely the class of event that makes a notifications system
    worth having.

  Deliberately out of scope for v1: drag reordering, bulk edit, undo UI (the log supports it; the buttons can come
  later), and anything notification-shaped. That last one is the discipline that makes next cycle clean.

  [1mDesign — Section 3: Testing[0m

  The module split is what makes this cheap. [38;5;153mevents[39m is pure functions over a log — table-driven tests, no setup. [38;5;153mtasks[39m
  tests against an in-memory [38;5;153mstore[39m implementation, which is the seam earning its keep on day one. [38;5;153mview[39m gets a thin smoke
  test that a render produces expected DOM; no heavy component testing for a single screen.

  The test I care most about: [1mfold the log, apply one more event, fold again — state matches.[0m That invariant is what
  notifications will depend on next cycle, and it's worth pinning before any of this grows.

  Since this is a new project with no tooling configured, one question — it's cheapest to decide now, before any code
  exists.

